\section{Introduction}
Black hole thermodynamics unifies general relativity, quantum field theory, and thermodynamics. 
Hawking's 1975 prediction that black holes radiate thermally was pivotal because it provided a finite temperature for an object classically expected to be perfectly cold.
The motivation of this work is twofold:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Why:} To revisit the Hawking temperature derivation in a way that exposes the essential reasoning steps from curvature to thermalization.
    \item \textbf{How:} By tracing surface gravity through the Unruh effect and Euclidean periodicity, yielding a clear physical pathway to the temperature formula.
    \item \textbf{What:} To extend that result into the UBP computational space, constructing a quantitative analog of surface gravity suitable for digital or symbolic physics simulation.
\end{itemize}

\section{Physical Foundation: The Schwarzschild Metric}
For a non-rotating, uncharged black hole, the Schwarzschild metric is
\begin{equation}
ds^2 = -\left(1-\frac{2GM}{c^2r}\right)c^2 dt^2 + 
\left(1-\frac{2GM}{c^2r}\right)^{-1}dr^2 + r^2 d\Omega^2,
\end{equation}
where \( G \) is the gravitational constant, \( c \) the speed of light, and \( M \) the black hole mass.

The event horizon occurs at the Schwarzschild radius
\begin{equation}
r_s = \frac{2GM}{c^2}.
\end{equation}

\section{Surface Gravity and the Origin of Temperature}
The \emph{surface gravity} \( \kappa \) defines the acceleration needed to remain stationary near the horizon. It is derived by expanding the metric coefficient near \( r_s \):
\begin{equation}
\kappa = \frac{c^4}{4GM}.
\end{equation}
This quantity carries units of acceleration (m\,s\(^{-2}\)) and determines the redshifted force per unit mass at the horizon.

Through the \emph{Unruh effect}, an observer with acceleration \( a \) perceives a temperature
\begin{equation}
T_U = \frac{\hbar a}{2\pi c k_B}.
\end{equation}
Substituting \( a = \kappa \) gives the Hawking temperature:
\begin{equation}
\boxed{T_H = \frac{\hbar c^3}{8\pi G M k_B}.}
\end{equation}

\subsection{Why this works}
The key insight is that spacetime curvature near the horizon produces the same vacuum structure as uniform acceleration in flat space. Virtual particle pairs that straddle the horizon appear as thermal radiation to distant observers.

\section{Euclidean Periodicity Argument (How)}
A mathematically rigorous route arises by requiring regularity of the metric under Wick rotation \( t \rightarrow i\tau \). Near \( r_s \), the Euclideanized metric demands periodicity of \(\tau\) with period \( 2\pi/\kappa \), enforcing a thermal factor:
\begin{equation}
T_H = \frac{\hbar \kappa}{2\pi k_B}.
\end{equation}
This method removes the need for field mode expansion, exposing the temperature as a geometric necessity.

