\section{Numerical Implementation and Residual Analysis}
\label{sec:numerics}

This section describes the numerical procedures used to compare the general-relativistic Hawking temperature
\(T_{\mathrm{GR}}\) with the UBP-mapped temperature \(T_{\mathrm{UBP}}\). The accompanying Python script computes both temperatures across a wide mass range,
plots the results on logarithmic axes, and shows fractional residuals.

\subsection{Definitions and Calibration}
We compute the standard Schwarzschild surface gravity and Hawking temperature:
\begin{align}
\kappa_{\mathrm{GR}}(M) &= \frac{c^4}{4GM}, \\
T_{\mathrm{GR}}(M) &= \frac{\hbar c^3}{8 \pi G M k_B}.
\end{align}

For the UBP mapping we use the dimensional calibration constant
\begin{equation}
K = \frac{c^4}{4G},
\end{equation}
so that a proposed UBP proxy for surface gravity is
\begin{equation}
\kappa_{\mathrm{UBP}}(M) = K\cdot S_{\mathrm{UBP}}(M),
\end{equation}
where \(S_{\mathrm{UBP}}(M)\) is a dimensionless resonance-derived function (OffBits-based). For this demonstration we adopt a linear-in-mass proxy
\(S_{\mathrm{UBP}}(M) = \alpha\,\dfrac{M}{M_\odot}\),
with calibration factor \(\alpha\) chosen such that
\(\kappa_{\mathrm{UBP}}(M_\odot)=\kappa_{\mathrm{GR}}(M_\odot)\).

Thus the mapped temperature is
\begin{equation}
T_{\mathrm{UBP}}(M) = \frac{\hbar\,\kappa_{\mathrm{UBP}}(M)}{2\pi c k_B}.
\end{equation}

\subsection{Metrics and Plots}
